==Prize money==
The distribution model split a total prize pool of US$1 billion between the 32 participating clubs. The winner of the tournament was awarded up to US$125 million, marking a significant increase in financial rewards compared to previous editions. In addition to the prize money for the participating teams, a solidarity investment program had a target of an additional US$250 million being provided to club football across the world.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/club-world-cup/usa-2025/articles/record-prize-money-solidarity |title=FIFA Club World Cup 2025: Record prize money and unprecedented solidarity to benefit club football|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|website=FIFA.com |date=March 26, 2025 |access-date=May 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250327195907/https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/club-world-cup/usa-2025/articles/record-prize-money-solidarity |archive-date=March 27, 2025| url-status=live}}</ref>

The distribution model for the 2025 edition was as follows:

'''Sporting performance pillar (US$475 million):''' Increasing payouts based on performance in the tournament.
* Group stage (three matches): + $2.0 million per win; + $1.0 million draw
* Round of 16: + $7.5 million
* Quarterfinal: + $13.125 million
* Semifinal: + $21.0 million
* Finalist: + $30.0 million
* Winner: + $40.0 million

'''Participation pillar (US$525 million):''' Guaranteed payments to all 32 clubs (amounts are per club).
* Europe: $12.81–38.19 million (determined by a ranking based on sporting and commercial criteria)
* South America: $15.21 million
* North, Central America & Caribbean: $9.55 million
* Asia: $9.55 million
* Africa: $9.55 million
* Oceania: $3.58 million

'''Solidarity payments (US$250 million):''' In addition to the prize money for the participating teams, these payments were allocated to support clubs worldwide, fostering development and inclusivity in global football.

==Broadcasting==
In July 2024, FIFA opened bids for media rights to the Club World Cup in the Americas, Asia, the Middle East and North Africa. It was reported that [[Apple Inc.|Apple]] (owner of the global rights to [[Major League Soccer]]) had attempted to make a $1 billion bid for global rights to the 2025 Club World Cup, but negotiations with FIFA had reportedly stalled; the bid was said to be a quarter of what FIFA had targeted for the rights to the tournament.<ref>{{cite news |date=July 15, 2024 |title=FIFA asks for Club World Cup broadcast bids just 11 months before 32-team event launches in US |url=https://apnews.com/article/club-world-cup-tv-rights-c093027faf888abd407d2dc39dbec8ae |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240809170259/https://apnews.com/article/club-world-cup-tv-rights-c093027faf888abd407d2dc39dbec8ae |archive-date=August 9, 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2024 |work=[[Associated Press News]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Crafton |first=Adam |date=July 15, 2024 |title=FIFA opens up bidding for Club World Cup media rights |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5639471/2024/07/15/fifa-club-world-cup-media-rights/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240809170259/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5639471/2024/07/15/fifa-club-world-cup-media-rights/ |archive-date=August 9, 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2024 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=McCaskill |first=Steve |date=July 16, 2024 |title=Fifa invites bids for regional rights to expanded Club World Cup |url=https://www.sportspro.com/news/fifa-club-world-cup-tv-deal-apple/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240716191457/https://www.sportspromedia.com/news/fifa-club-world-cup-tv-deal-apple/ |archive-date=July 16, 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2024 |website=SportsPro}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=June 21, 2024 |title=Low financial incentives and lack of broadcast partner plaguing FIFA Club World Cup |url=https://www.sportsbusinessjournal.com/Articles/2024/06/21/fifa-club-world-cup-issues |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240819164023/https://www.sportsbusinessjournal.com/Articles/2024/06/21/fifa-club-world-cup-issues |archive-date=August 19, 2024 |access-date=August 19, 2024 |website=Sports Business Journal}}</ref> On September 19, 2024, FIFA called an emergency meeting to discuss broadcasting rights, as it had not yet reached deals in a number of major markets.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5779549/2024/09/19/fifa-club-world-cup-broadcast-rights/|title=Infantino calls emergency meeting with broadcasters over lack of FIFA Club World Cup deal|first=Adam|last=Crafton|date=September 19, 2024|work=The New York Times|access-date=September 20, 2024|archive-date=September 20, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240920183423/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5779549/2024/09/19/fifa-club-world-cup-broadcast-rights/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On December 4, 2024, [[DAZN]] acquired global rights to the tournament for €1 billion, streaming all matches for free. DAZN would also collaborate with Host Broadcasting Services (HBS) on aspects of the world feed production.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Costa |first=Brandon |title=DAZN Brings Global Ambition to U.S. Soil With Production of 2025 FIFA Club World Cup |url=https://www.sportsvideo.org/2025/06/17/dazn-brings-global-ambition-to-u-s-soil-with-production-of-2025-fifa-club-world-cup/ |access-date=2025-10-16 |website=Sports Video Group |language=en}}</ref> In some regions, DAZN sublicensed coverage to linear television broadcasters.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fifa Club World Cup 2025: DAZN secures exclusive broadcast rights |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/crl387gr18ro |website=BBC Sport |access-date=December 4, 2024 |date=December 4, 2024}}</ref> In the United States, DAZN reached sub-licensing and co-production agreements with [[Warner Bros. Discovery]]'s [[TNT Sports (United States)|TNT Sports]] (English) and [[TelevisaUnivision]]'s [[TUDN (brand)|TUDN]] (Spanish), under which their respective properties would hold exclusive rights to portions of the tournament. The two divisions would co-produce studio programming for the tournament seen across their properties and DAZN, with TNT Sports originating English-language coverage from its studios in Atlanta.<ref name=":1" />

Media reports suggested that DAZN struggled to find a buyer in the UK, as the [[BBC]] was unwilling to sublicense for fear of promoting a commercial broadcaster, and [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] had attempted to obtain the rights for free before the deal was signed with DAZN.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wallace |first=Sam |date=2025-03-01 |title=ITV in £0 bid to broadcast Club World Cup |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2025/03/01/itv-bids-zero-pounds-club-world-cup/ |access-date=2026-03-02 |work=The Telegraph |language=en-GB |issn=0307-1235}}</ref> The rights were eventually sublicensed to [[5 (British TV channel)|5]], which enjoyed strong ratings in the 16–34 demographic.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Miller |first=Max |title=2.4m watch Club World Cup final on 5 |url=https://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/broadcasting/24m-watch-club-world-cup-final-on-5/5207029.article |access-date=2026-03-02 |website=Broadcast |language=en}}</ref>

On April 10, 2025, Chinese streaming platform [[China Mobile Communications|Migu]] acquired broadcast rights in China to the 2025 and 2029 Club World Cup. Since DAZN does not operate in China, it was understood that FIFA negotiated the deal with Migu directly in partnership with the [[Chinese Football Association]].<ref name="Migu nets Club World Cup in direct Fifa deal">{{cite web |date=April 10, 2025 |title=Migu nets Club World Cup in direct FIFA deal |url=https://www.sportbusiness.com/news/migu-nets-club-world-cup-in-direct-fifa-deal/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250411095233/https://www.sportbusiness.com/news/migu-nets-club-world-cup-in-direct-fifa-deal/ |archive-date=April 11, 2025 |access-date=April 11, 2025 |website=SportBusiness}}</ref> On June 11, 2025, [[Coupang Play]] in [[South Korea]] announced it would provide coverage of the FIFA Club World Cup under a separate pricing plan to its basic plan.<ref>{{cite news |author={{Lang|ko|이정현}} |date=June 11, 2025 |script-title=ko:쿠팡플레이, FIFA 클럽월드컵 선택형 부가 서비스로 중계 |url=https://www.yna.co.kr/view/AKR20250611103600017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250611072426/https://www.yna.co.kr/view/AKR20250611103600017 |archive-date=June 11, 2025 |access-date=June 11, 2025 |work=[[Yonhap News Agency]] |language=ko}}</ref> In the days prior to the tournament, [[Sportradar]] announced agreements to serve as the exclusive provider of real-time sports betting data for the tournament, expanding upon existing agreements with FIFA to provide match integrity services. The agreement also included non-exclusive rights to distribute DAZN's coverage of the tournament via sports betting platforms.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cunningham |first=Euan |date=2025-06-12 |title=Sportradar in major deal with DAZN for CWC betting data rights |url=https://www.sportcal.com/betting/sportradar-in-major-deal-with-dazn-for-cwc-betting-data-rights/ |access-date=2025-10-16 |website=Sportcal |language=en-US}}</ref>

