==Reception==
{{Criticism section|date=July 2025}}

===Impact of added European fixtures===
The proposed expansion of the FIFA Club World Cup drew criticism from several football stakeholders, including [[FIFPRO]], a global union representing players from 66 associations, and the [[World Leagues Forum]], which represents 47 professional leagues, both citing concerns about player welfare and fixture congestion.<ref>{{cite news |last=Grohmann |first=Karolos |date=December 16, 2022 |title=Club World Cup to feature 32 teams in 2025, says FIFA president Infantino |url=https://www.reuters.com/lifestyle/sports/club-world-cup-feature-32-teams-2025-says-fifa-president-infantino-2022-12-16/ |work=[[Reuters]] |accessdate=December 18, 2022 |archive-date=March 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321202429/https://www.reuters.com/lifestyle/sports/club-world-cup-feature-32-teams-2025-says-fifa-president-infantino-2022-12-16/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="newformat"/> [[La Liga]] also voiced opposition and threatened legal action.<ref>{{cite news |date=December 17, 2022 |title=Spanish league attacks FIFA's plan for bigger Club World Cup |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/soccer/spanish-league-attacks-fifas-plan-for-bigger-club-world-cup/2022/12/17/592fe95e-7e26-11ed-bb97-f47d47466b9a_story.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=December 18, 2022}}</ref> Critics accused FIFA of prioritising commercial interests over player health.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://onefootball.com/en/news/fifa-accused-of-prioritising-own-interests-after-revealing-club-world-cup-plans-38740610|title=FIFA accused of prioritising own interests after revealing Club World Cup plans|work=OneFootball.com|date=December 17, 2023|access-date=December 19, 2023}}</ref> The introduction of the annual [[FIFA Intercontinental Cup]] was similarly criticised.<ref name="Guardian-May2024">{{cite news |last=MacInnes |first=Paul |date=May 9, 2024 |title=Fifa must postpone Club World Cup or face legal action, warns players' union |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/may/09/fifa-must-postpone-club-world-cup-or-face-legal-action-warns-players-union |work=The Guardian |accessdate=May 11, 2024}}</ref> In May 2024, FIFPRO and the World Leagues Forum sent a letter to FIFA demanding rescheduling and reform of the [[FIFA International Match Calendar]] and warned of legal or other action if their concerns were ignored.<ref name="Guardian-May2024"/><ref>{{cite news |date=May 9, 2024 |title=Club World Cup: Why FIFA is facing pushback over US-staged tournament |url=https://apnews.com/article/fifa-club-world-cup-1a8a852dd1c3892737a757fd40fd7f8b |access-date=August 19, 2024 |website=AP News |archive-date=August 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240819162230/https://apnews.com/article/fifa-club-world-cup-1a8a852dd1c3892737a757fd40fd7f8b |url-status=live }}</ref> On June 13, 2024, the English [[Professional Footballers' Association]] and France's [[Union Nationale des Footballeurs Professionnels]] filed a legal claim in the [[Commercial Tribunal (Belgium)|Business Court]] in [[Brussels]] to protest the tournament.<ref>{{cite web |agency=[[PA Media]] |title=Footballers' union starts legal action against Fifa over Club World Cup |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/jun/13/footballers-union-fifpro-legal-action-fifa-club-world-cup |website=The Guardian |access-date=June 14, 2024 |date=June 13, 2024}}</ref> FIFPRO also warned of potential strike action.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://apnews.com/article/club-world-cup-player-strike-17f5f3644dfddbec0132445a54bc72eb | title=Club World Cup pushback sees the possibility of player strike action raised again | date=November 21, 2024 | work=Associated Press }}</ref> On January 30, 2025, a meeting between FIFA and FIFPRO was held to address fixture congestion.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fifpro meet with Fifa over fixture congestion concerns |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c20kgrz4k30o |website=BBC Sport |access-date=February 9, 2025 |date=January 30, 2025 |archive-date=February 4, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250204003459/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c20kgrz4k30o |url-status=live }}</ref> In response to the criticism, FIFA stated that the Club World Cup's schedule followed the international calendar and allowed for rest before domestic competitions, adding that its events occupied just 1 percent of the football calendar and were agreed with players and confederations.<ref name="oneyear">{{cite news |last=Crafton |first=Adam |date=June 7, 2024 |title=One year until the Club World Cup in the United States - what's going on? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5544103/2024/06/07/club-world-cup-united-states-soccer/ |access-date=August 19, 2024 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=August 17, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240817134236/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5544103/2024/06/07/club-world-cup-united-states-soccer/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=October 14, 2024 |title=FIFA's Response to Players' Complaint |url=https://www.beinsports.com/en-us/soccer/articles-video/fifa-s-response-to-players-complaint-2024-10-14 |url-status=unfit |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250623103318/https://www.beinsports.com/en-us/soccer/articles-video/fifa-s-response-to-players-complaint-2024-10-14 |archive-date=June 23, 2025 |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=beIN Sports}}</ref>

===Impact of multi-club ownership===
Several clubs in the 2025 FIFA Club World Cup were linked to [[Multi-club ownership (association football)|multi-club ownership]] structures, raising concerns about conflicts of interest. Among them were Mexican sides [[C.F. Pachuca|Pachuca]] and [[Club León|León]], both owned by Grupo Pachuca. FIFA regulations prohibit any club from owning shares in, being a member of, or exerting influence over another participating club, with "influence" defined through control of voting rights or decision-making authority.<ref name="regulations" /> In November 2024, Costa Rican club [[Liga Deportiva Alajuelense|Alajuelense]] requested FIFA to apply this rule and disqualify one of the two Grupo Pachuca clubs, threatening legal action if no action was taken.<ref>{{cite news |date=November 20, 2024 |title=Costa Rica's Alajuelense threatens legal action over Club World Cup spot |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/42509544/alajuelense-threatens-legal-action-club-world-cup-spot |publisher=ESPN |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=November 20, 2024 |archive-date=November 20, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241120213944/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/42509544/alajuelense-threatens-legal-action-club-world-cup-spot |url-status=live }}</ref> Alajuelense argued that it was next in line to qualify under CONCACAF's rankings, but FIFA determined the club could not be party to proceedings.<ref>{{cite web |last=Lawton |first=Matt |date=December 2, 2024 |title=Fifa closes door on Costa Rican side challenging Club World Cup rules |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/fifa-hits-back-at-accusations-of-breaking-own-club-world-cup-rules-2ktwtdpm2 |access-date=March 23, 2025 |website=The Times |url-access=subscription |archive-date=January 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250115213802/https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/fifa-hits-back-at-accusations-of-breaking-own-club-world-cup-rules-2ktwtdpm2 |url-status=live }}</ref> On February 4, 2025, Alajuelense appealed to the [[Court of Arbitration for Sport]] against FIFA, Pachuca, and León.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/club-world-cup-fifa-court-arbitration-sport-alajuelense-3ng9p6kk3|title=Costa Rican club takes Fifa to court over 'breaking' Club World Cup rules|first=Matt|last=Lawton|date=February 3, 2025|website=The Times|url-access=subscription|access-date=February 4, 2025|archive-date=February 9, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250209210506/https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/club-world-cup-fifa-court-arbitration-sport-alajuelense-3ng9p6kk3|url-status=live}}</ref> On March 21, FIFA announced that León had been removed from the tournament.<ref name="FIFALeonAppeal" /> According to ''[[The Guardian]]'', a play-off would determine the replacement between [[Los Angeles FC]], runner-up of the 2023 CONCACAF Champions League, and [[Club América|América]], the highest-ranked Mexican club not already qualified.<ref>{{cite news |last=Abnos |first=Alexander |date=March 31, 2025 |title=Fifa considering LAFC v Club América playoff to replace León at Club World Cup |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/mar/31/fifa-considering-lafc-v-club-america-playoff-to-replace-leon-at-club-world-cup |access-date=April 8, 2025 |work=The Guardian |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Alajuelense and the [[Philadelphia Union]] were not considered. León appealed, and hearings for both its case and Alajuelense's took place in April and May 2025 respectively, but on May 6 FIFA confirmed that León's expulsion would stand and that the play-off would proceed.<ref name="FIFA-Playoff" /> LAFC secured the final spot with a 2–1 extra time win over América on May 31 at [[BMO Stadium]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Labidou |first=Alex |url=https://www.goal.com/en/lists/denis-bouanga-lafc-club-america-richest-north-america-soccer-match-to-book-2025-club-world-cup-ticket/blt6f961607f1ca6df4 |title=Denis Bouanga delivers in extra time as LAFC beat Club América in richest North America soccer match to book 2025 Club World Cup ticket |website=Goal.com |date=June 1, 2025 |access-date=June 1, 2025 |archive-date=June 29, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250629003016/https://www.goal.com/en/lists/denis-bouanga-lafc-club-america-richest-north-america-soccer-match-to-book-2025-club-world-cup-ticket/blt6f961607f1ca6df4 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Ticket prices and attendance===
[[File:2025 FIFA Club World Cup - River Plate vs. Urawa Red Diamonds - 01.jpg|thumb|The group stage game between [[Club Atlético River Plate|River Plate]] and [[Urawa Red Diamonds]] in [[Seattle]] drew 11,974 spectators]]
Ticket prices for the 2025 FIFA Club World Cup attracted early scrutiny from some fans, who noted that certain final seats were listed at over US$2,200 due to dynamic pricing models,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/fifas-us-bonanza-begins-with-alarming-club-world-cup-ticket-prices-adaptive-pricing-164042681.html|title=FIFA's U.S. bonanza begins with 'alarming' Club World Cup ticket prices, 'adaptive pricing'|date=December 19, 2024|website=Yahoo Sports}}</ref> although FIFA later adjusted prices in February 2025, lowering them to around US$140 for the semifinals and US$300 for the final to make the tournament more accessible.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.insideworldfootball.com/2025/02/10/fifa-slashes-club-world-cup-ticket-prices-fans-fail-buy-hype|title=FIFA slashes Club World Cup ticket prices as fans fail to buy the hype|date=February 10, 2025|access-date=February 10, 2025|archive-date=February 12, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250212162948/https://www.insideworldfootball.com/2025/02/10/fifa-slashes-club-world-cup-ticket-prices-fans-fail-buy-hype/|url-status=live}}</ref> A 10 percent cancellation fee applied to exchanges also drew attention,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.insideworldfootball.com/2025/02/13/bayern-fans-irate-fifa-club-world-club-ticket-pricing-cancellation-fees/|title=Bayern fans irate at FIFA Club World Club ticket pricing and cancellation fees|date=February 13, 2025|access-date=February 13, 2025|archive-date=February 13, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250213163521/https://www.insideworldfootball.com/2025/02/13/bayern-fans-irate-fifa-club-world-club-ticket-pricing-cancellation-fees/|url-status=live}}</ref> but the overall response to the tournament was positive. The opening fixture between [[Al Ahly SC|Al Ahly]] and [[Inter Miami CF|Inter Miami]] saw strong turnout following final-week promotional campaigns,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/josephosullivan/2025/06/15/ticket-trouble-and-zero-goals-at-the-fifa-2025-club-world-cup-opener/|title=Ticket Trouble And Zero Goals At The FIFA 2025 Club World Cup Opener |work=[[Forbes]]|date=June 15, 2025|access-date=June 15, 2025|archive-date=June 15, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615205159/https://www.forbes.com/sites/josephosullivan/2025/06/15/ticket-trouble-and-zero-goals-at-the-fifa-2025-club-world-cup-opener/|url-status=live}}</ref> and FIFA reported over 60,000 spectators in attendance, with strong international broadcast figures.<ref>{{cite news |title=By the numbers: The first round of the FIFA Club World Cup 2025 |url=https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/news/by-the-numbers-first-round-club-world-cup-2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620023348/https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/news/by-the-numbers-first-round-club-world-cup-2025 |archive-date=June 20, 2025|access-date=June 20, 2025|date=June 19, 2025|publisher=FIFA |website=FIFA.com}}</ref> By early July, FIFA had recorded over 2 million tickets sold and average attendance of approximately 36,000 per match,<ref>{{cite news |title=Eyes of the world: FIFA Club World Cup 2025 quarter-finals to feature global icons representing billions |url=https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/news/club-world-cup-stats-1-4-final-stats-goals-countries-attendances |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250702175451/https://inside.fifa.com/organisation/news/club-world-cup-stats-1-4-final-stats-goals-countries-attendances |archive-date=July 2, 2025 |access-date=July 2, 2025 |website=FIFA Inside}}</ref> a figure comparable to several leading European domestic leagues.<ref name="guardian_numbers">{{cite web |date=June 5, 2025 |title=The Club World Cup 2025 by numbers: attendances, heat and head-to-heads |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/jul/04/club-world-cup-by-numbers-attendances-heat-and-head-to-heads |access-date=July 4, 2025 |website=The Guardian |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250704190557/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/jul/04/club-world-cup-by-numbers-attendances-heat-and-head-to-heads |archive-date= July 4, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> While 14 matches attracted smaller crowds of under 20,000, mostly at MLS venues, 11 fixtures surpassed 90 percent capacity, including six at Hard Rock Stadium.<ref name="guardian_numbers"/> Supporters from South America, Africa and Asia were especially prominent throughout the tournament,<ref name="ESPN-Important">{{cite web |date=June 19, 2025 |title=Is the Club World Cup important? Yes, just ask the South American teams |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/45540511/why-fifa-club-world-cup-means-much-south-american-teams |access-date=June 23, 2025 |publisher=ESPN |archive-date=June 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623232137/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/45540511/why-fifa-club-world-cup-means-much-south-american-teams |url-status=live }}</ref> although a match involving [[Wydad AC|Wydad]] and [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] saw minor incidents involving smoke bombs and one arrest.<ref>{{cite news |last=Tom |first=Ignudo |date=June 23, 2025 |title=1 arrested, 7 ejected at Wydad AC-Juventus FIFA Club World Cup match after fans spotted setting off smoke bombs |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/wydad-ac-vs-juventus-philadelphia-fifa-club-world-cup/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=CBS News |archive-date=June 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623144218/https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/wydad-ac-vs-juventus-philadelphia-fifa-club-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Yamashita |first=Andy |date=June 17, 2025 |title=International soccer fans bring lively atmosphere to Seattle for Club World Cup |url=https://www.seattletimes.com/sports/world-cup/international-soccer-fans-bring-lively-atmosphere-to-seattle-for-club-world-cup/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |work=The Seattle Times |archive-date=June 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623232137/https://www.seattletimes.com/sports/world-cup/international-soccer-fans-bring-lively-atmosphere-to-seattle-for-club-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Selection of Inter Miami CF===
As with previous editions of the FIFA Club World Cup, the expanded 2025 format allocated a slot for the host nation.<ref name="slots" /> This slot had traditionally been filled by the domestic league champion of the host country. In the United States, the champion of [[Major League Soccer]] is determined through the [[MLS Cup playoffs]], rather than regular season performance, and for the 2024 season, that playoff had not yet concluded at the time FIFA needed to confirm its selection.<ref name="miami yahoo">{{cite news|last=Bushnell|first=Henry|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/fifa-gives-lionel-messi-inter-miami-a-spot-in-2025-club-world-cup-002919588.html|title=FIFA gives Lionel Messi, Inter Miami a spot in 2025 Club World Cup|work=Yahoo Sports|date=October 19, 2024|access-date=November 11, 2024|archive-date=November 11, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241111062221/https://sports.yahoo.com/fifa-gives-lionel-messi-inter-miami-a-spot-in-2025-club-world-cup-002919588.html|url-status=live}}</ref> During the mid-season [[2024 MLS All-Star Game]], MLS Commissioner [[Don Garber]] stated that the slot could be awarded to the 2024 [[MLS Supporters' Shield]] winner, the [[MLS Cup 2024]] winner, or via a playoff between the two.<ref name="MLS CWC">{{cite web|url=https://www.mlssoccer.com/news/mls-commissioner-don-garber-2024-mls-all-star-game-media-availability-quote-sheet|title=MLS Commissioner Don Garber: 2024 MLS All-Star Game Media Availability Quote Sheet|website=MLSsoccer.com|date=July 25, 2024|access-date=November 11, 2024|archive-date=November 11, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241111200157/https://www.mlssoccer.com/news/mls-commissioner-don-garber-2024-mls-all-star-game-media-availability-quote-sheet|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="tata athletic">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5733735/2024/08/30/martino-mls-supporters-shield-fifa-world-cup/|title=Gerardo Martino targets MLS Supporters' Shield as route into FIFA Club World Cup|last=Cardenas|first=Felipe|work=The Athletic|url-access=subscription|date=August 30, 2024|access-date=November 11, 2024|archive-date=November 11, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241111200520/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5733735/2024/08/30/martino-mls-supporters-shield-fifa-world-cup/|url-status=live}}</ref> On October 19, 2024, FIFA announced that [[Inter Miami CF|Inter Miami]] would represent the host nation as winners of the Supporters' Shield, awarding the slot before the start of the [[2024 MLS Cup playoffs]] and after Inter Miami had already secured the Shield.<ref name="miami qual" /> FIFA stated that this decision aligned with past precedent, where clubs that won silverware in a league-style format were prioritised for the host slot, and that the announcement had been delayed due to procedural timelines for confirming regulations.<ref name="CBS Sports Tom Bogert">{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qZWYtADvDy8|title=Tom Bogert talks playoffs, Messi vs. Cucho, FIFA's non-transparent process, Season Best XI|website=YouTube|date=October 22, 2024|access-date=April 17, 2025|archive-date=April 20, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420113645/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qZWYtADvDy8|url-status=live}}</ref> Although the decision drew scrutiny from some sections of the media and fanbase, particularly regarding its timing and the involvement of [[Lionel Messi]],<ref name="miami yahoo"/><ref name="miami athletic">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5856541/2024/10/20/lionel-messi-miami-club-world-cup/|title=Lionel Messi at the Club World Cup makes sense – but how he got there is ridiculous|last=Spiers|first=Tim|work=The Athletic|url-access=subscription|date=October 20, 2024|access-date=November 11, 2024|archive-date=November 11, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241111195227/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5856541/2024/10/20/lionel-messi-miami-club-world-cup/|url-status=live}}</ref> others viewed it as a practical resolution in keeping with the tournament's structure and historical practice. Inter Miami were later eliminated from the playoffs by [[Atlanta United FC|Atlanta United]] in the [[2024 MLS Cup playoffs#Round one|first round]] on November 10,<ref name="miami reuters">{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/club-world-cup-slot-softens-blow-miami-defeat-2024-11-11/|title=Club World Cup slot softens the blow of Miami defeat|last=Phillips|first=Mitch|work=Reuters|date=November 11, 2024|access-date=November 11, 2024}}</ref> but head coach [[Gerardo Martino]] defended the club's inclusion on the grounds that the Supporters' Shield constituted a valid and recognised form of domestic achievement.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/41608519/inter-miami-deserves-club-world-cup-invite-martino|title=Inter Miami deserves Club World Cup invite – Martino|last=Becherano|first=Lizzy|website=espn.com|date=October 4, 2024|access-date=November 13, 2024|archive-date=November 16, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241116151329/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/41608519/inter-miami-deserves-club-world-cup-invite-martino|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Immigration and visa issues ===
On June 10, 2025, [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) and [[U.S. Customs and Border Protection|Customs and Border Protection]] (CBP) announced their involvement in providing security during the first round of the 2025 FIFA Club World Cup and reminded non-American citizens to carry valid proof of legal stay. The announcement raised concerns among some fans regarding the [[Deportation in the second presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration's deportation policy]], especially as those detained could potentially face indefinite detention or be transferred to other "[[Third-country resettlement|third countries]]", such as [[El Salvador]].<ref name="iceagents">{{cite news |last=Odzer |first=Ari |date=June 10, 2025 |title=Questions raised about ICE and CBP agents at Club World Cup games |url=https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/questions-raised-about-ice-and-cbp-agents-at-club-world-cup-games/3634343/ |archive-date=June 15, 2025 |access-date=June 12, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615135541/https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/questions-raised-about-ice-and-cbp-agents-at-club-world-cup-games/3634343/ |url-status=live }}</ref> FIFA President Gianni Infantino defended the increased security presence, stating that the safety of fans was a top priority, particularly in light of the [[2024 Copa América final]], which was held in the same stadium and saw large numbers of supporters storm the gates to gain entry.<ref name="iceagents" /> Just days later, on June 15, reports surfaced that [[Boca Juniors]] defender [[Ayrton Costa]] had initially been denied a US visa due to a 2018 robbery case, although the decision was subsequently reversed and he was issued a special 26-day visa to participate in the tournament.<ref>{{cite web |last=LaSota |first=Mark |title=2025 FIFA Club World Cup: Vision, Competition, And Controversy |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/marklasota/2025/06/15/2025-fifa-club-world-cup-vision-competition-and-controversy/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |date=June 15, 2025 |website=Forbes |archive-date=June 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624013633/https://www.forbes.com/sites/marklasota/2025/06/15/2025-fifa-club-world-cup-vision-competition-and-controversy/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Rivalry between Europe and South America ===
The tournament intensified the rivalry between European teams and the rest of the world, in particular the South Americans.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sanderson |first1=Tom |last2=Seixas |first2=Josué |date=June 27, 2025 |title=Brazilian teams have excelled at the Club World Cup. How far can they go? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/jun/27/brazilian-teams-club-world-cup-flamengo-botafogo-palmeiras-fluminense |access-date=July 2, 2025 |work=The Guardian|issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Initially the debate involved how well the non-European teams would fare against the European teams; it then intensified when South American teams began to record victories against their European counterparts. Some South American managers, fans, and journalists also tried to dismiss European criticism of the tournament as "[[Sore loser|loser excuses]]" and a "symbol of [[Eurocentrism|European arrogance]] and [[elitism]]".<ref>{{cite web |date=June 20, 2025 |title='Não tem desculpa!': Renato Gaúcho faz desabafo fortíssimo sobre Brasil x Europa no Mundial |trans-title='There has no excuses!': Fluminense manager spills out about Europeans on Club World Cup |url=https://www.espn.com.br/futebol/fluminense/artigo/_/id/15335080/renato-gaucho-faz-desabafo-fortissimo-brasil-x-europa-mundial-nao-tem-desculpa |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=ESPN Brazil |language=pt-br |archive-date=June 22, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250622044921/https://www.espn.com.br/futebol/fluminense/artigo/_/id/15335080/renato-gaucho-faz-desabafo-fortissimo-brasil-x-europa-mundial-nao-tem-desculpa |url-status=live }}</ref> Commentators questioned whether European dominance had been overstated, with analysis suggesting that the myth of superiority was being tested by results on the pitch.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bushnell |first=Henry |date=June 21, 2025 |title=Club World Cup: Is European soccer's superiority being exposed as a myth? |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/article/club-world-cup-is-European-soccers-superiority-being-exposed-as-a-myth-040219007.html |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Yahoo Sports |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624114711/https://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/article/club-world-cup-is-european-soccers-superiority-being-exposed-as-a-myth-040219007.html |archive-date=June 24, 2025 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Lang |first=Jack |date=June 23, 2025 |title=What Brazil's early success at the Club World Cup says of its status as a football power |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6444353/2025/06/23/brazilian-club-world-cup/ |access-date=June 24, 2025 |work=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=June 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624011119/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6444353/2025/06/23/brazilian-club-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In response, [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] coach [[Luis Enrique]] remarked that South Americans had no need to compare themselves with Europeans, emphasising the shared global stature of both footballing regions and pointing to the widespread recruitment of South American players by European clubs.<ref>{{cite web |date=June 23, 2025 |title=Técnico do PSG questiona 'rivalidade' dos sul-americanos com Europeus |trans-title=PSG coach questions South Americans' 'rivalry' with Europeans |url=https://www.uol.com.br/esporte/futebol/ultimas-noticias/2025/06/23/tecnico-psg-mundial-sul-americanos-Europeus.htm |website=UOL Brazil |language=pt-br |access-date=June 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624011119/https://www.uol.com.br/esporte/futebol/ultimas-noticias/2025/06/23/tecnico-psg-mundial-sul-americanos-europeus.htm |archive-date=June 24, 2025 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=June 23, 2025 |title=Luis Enrique diz não entender comparação sul-americanos x Europeus no Mundial: 'Muito preocupados com isso' |url=https://www.espn.com.br/futebol/mundial-de-clubes/artigo/_/id/15345148/mundial-de-clubes-luis-enrique-psg-brasileiros-dao-importancia-vencer-Europeus-muito-preocupados-com-isso |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=ESPN |language=pt |archive-date=June 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250623124620/https://www.espn.com.br/futebol/mundial-de-clubes/artigo/_/id/15345148/mundial-de-clubes-luis-enrique-psg-brasileiros-dao-importancia-vencer-europeus-muito-preocupados-com-isso |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] manager [[Pep Guardiola]] offered a more supportive view of South American competitiveness, arguing that European fans should not be surprised when clubs from the region secure wins and that such outcomes reflect the real global balance of quality in football.<ref>{{cite news |last=Campbell |first=Jordan |date=June 22, 2025 |title=Manchester City manager Pep Guardiola open to coaching in South America in future: 'Why not?' |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6442587/2025/06/21/pep-guardiola-south-america-job/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250622005120/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6442587/2025/06/21/pep-guardiola-south-america-job/ |archive-date=June 22, 2025 |access-date=June 23, 2025 |work=The Athletic |via=The New York Times |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> However, [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] coach [[Enzo Maresca]] argued that European clubs faced congested schedules that could affect player fitness, but a journalist noted that South American teams played even more matches during the same period, challenging the idea that European sides were uniquely overworked.<ref>{{cite web |last=Gonzalez |first=Dante |url=https://worldsoccertalk.com/news/enzo-maresca-spars-with-journalist-over-chelseas-packed-schedule-ahead-of-club-world-cup-game-vs-fluminense/ |title=Enzo Maresca spars with journalist over Chelsea's packed schedule ahead of Club World Cup game vs. Fluminense |publisher=World Soccer Talk |date=7 July 2025 }}</ref>

The increased attention to Brazilian clubs also led to a sharp spike in global interest in the [[Campeonato Brasileiro Série A|Brazilian League]], with [[Google Trends]] reporting the highest search volume for the term since 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last=Pereira |first=Vitoria |date=June 27, 2025 |title=Buscas por 'brazilian league' disparam mundialmente com quatro times no Mundial de Clubes |trans-title=Searches for 'Brazilian League' boosted worldwide with the four teams in the CWC |url=https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/esporte/2025/06/buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-mundialmente-com-quatro-times-no-mundial-de-clubes.shtml |access-date=July 4, 2025 |website=Folha de S.Paulo |language=pt-BR |archive-date=July 4, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250704044525/https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/esporte/2025/06/buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-mundialmente-com-quatro-times-no-mundial-de-clubes.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Consolin |first=Beatriz |date=June 24, 2025 |title=Mundial: buscas por "brazilian league" disparam após sucesso de brasileiros |trans-title=Searches for 'Brazilian League' are boosted after success of Brazilian Teams |url=https://www.cnnbrasil.com.br/esportes/futebol/mundial-buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-apos-sucesso-de-brasileiros/ |access-date=July 4, 2025 |website=CNN Brazil |language=pt-BR |archive-date=July 4, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250704103431/https://www.cnnbrasil.com.br/esportes/futebol/mundial-buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-apos-sucesso-de-brasileiros/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Chatelain |first=Christian |date=June 24, 2025 |title=Após vitórias históricas, buscas por "brazilian league" disparam no mundo durante o Mundial de Clubes |url=https://www.gizmodo.com.br/apos-vitorias-historicas-buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-no-mundo-durante-o-mundial-de-clubes-18936 |access-date=July 4, 2025 |website=Gizmodo |language=pt-BR |archive-date=July 4, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250704103431/https://www.gizmodo.com.br/apos-vitorias-historicas-buscas-por-brazilian-league-disparam-no-mundo-durante-o-mundial-de-clubes-18936 |url-status=live }}</ref> While the performances reignited continental pride, the on-field results remained competitive; in twelve direct UEFA–CONMEBOL encounters, European clubs registered six wins, South American clubs won three times, and three matches ended in draws.<ref name="guardian_numbers" /><ref name="rsssf_cwc2025">{{cite web |title=FIFA Club World Cup 2025 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fifa-wcc2025.html |website=rsssf.org |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |date=21 July 2025 |access-date=7 August 2025}}</ref>

=== Weather and logistical challenges ===
The tournament was marked by significant weather-related and logistical disruptions, raising concerns ahead of the [[2026 FIFA World Cup]]. Several matches were delayed or temporarily suspended due to [[thunderstorms]] and [[lightning]], with stoppages ranging from around 46 minutes to nearly two hours, as FIFA safety protocols required repeated pauses whenever lightning was detected near stadiums.<ref>{{cite web |last=Poole |first=Claire |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/clairepoolesp/2025/06/29/the-cost-of-extreme-weather-delays-at-fifa-club-world-cup/ |title=The Cost Of Extreme Weather Delays At FIFA Club World Cup |website=Forbes |date=29 June 2025 }}</ref> High temperatures also posed a challenge, particularly for midday kickoffs, with players and coaches highlighting the physical toll of extreme heat and uneven playing conditions between day and evening matches.<ref>{{cite web |last=Gruet |first=Sam |url=https://www.bbc.com/worklife/article/20251203-how-extreme-heat-could-disrupt-the-2026-world-cup |title=How extreme heat could disrupt the 2026 World Cup |website=BBC Worklife |date=4 December 2025 }}</ref> Beyond the pitch, severe weather contributed to travel and traffic problems, including flight delays and congestion around venues, affecting teams, officials, media, and supporters, further intensifying debate about scheduling and infrastructure preparedness for future tournaments.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com.au/football/story/_/id/45732808/2026-fifa-world-cup-lessons-2025-fifa-club-world-cup-weather-traffic-more |title=2026 World Cup lessons from the 2025 Club World Cup: Weather, traffic and more |website=ESPN |date=16 July 2025 }}</ref>

=== Financial distribution and governance concerns ===

The tournament faced criticism over delays in distributing £185 million in promised solidarity payments to non-participating clubs. While participating teams received significant prize money, including around £84 million for winners Chelsea, smaller clubs and leagues questioned the lack of clarity surrounding payment formulas and timelines, renewing debates about financial inequality and transparency in FIFA's expanded competitions.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Ames |first1=Nick |last2=Hughes |first2=Matt |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/feb/19/clubs-wait-fifa-share-185m-club-world-cup-money-solidarity-payments |title=Clubs frustrated over wait for Fifa to share £185m of Club World Cup money |publisher=The Guardian |date=19 February 2026 }}</ref> These concerns were brought up again in August 2026 following the withdrawn [[FIFA Forward Enterprise]] proposal.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Alcheva |first1=Martina |title=Gianni Infantino faces another tournament headache as FIFA’s 2029 Club World Cup plan hits two major obstacles |url=https://worldsoccertalk.com/news/gianni-infantino-faces-another-tournament-headache-as-fifas-2029-club-world-cup-plan-hits-two-major-obstacles/ |access-date=7 August 2026 |work=World Soccer Talk |date=4 August 2026}}</ref>

